# SYNOPSIS

```usage
getconf [-v spec] var [path]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`getconf` prints the value of the configuration variable <var>, and a newline.

With no <path>, <var> is looked for first among the names `sysconf` takes, without their `_SC_` prefix, as `ARG_MAX` or `PAGESIZE`, and the value asked of the C library; then among those `confstr` takes, as `PATH`; then among the limits of `<limits.h>`: the `_POSIX_` minimums such as `_POSIX_PATH_MAX`, whose values were fixed when `getconf` was built.
With a <path>, <var> is one of the names `pathconf` takes, as `NAME_MAX`, and the value is asked for <path>.
A value the C library calls indeterminate is printed as `undefined`.

The names are those in `ports/sbase/generated/getconf.h`, made from sbase's `scripts/getconf.sh` when sbase was vendored, and kept where musl's headers define them.

: `-v` <spec>
  Accepted and ignored: there is one programming environment.

`getconf` is sbase's, and found only in the POSIX namespace: `/boot/bin/posix/getconf` is a hard link to `sbase-box`, which runs the command its name names, and `/lib/ns/posix` (a manifest's `ns=posix`, see namespace(6)) binds `/boot/bin/posix` before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program that runs `getconf` finds it there.
There is no native `getconf`.

# SEE ALSO

uname(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/getconf.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

A <var> it does not know is reported on standard error, as `getconf: invalid variable: NOTHING`, with exit status 1.
A failed call is reported with the system's reason, exit status 1.
No <var>, more than two arguments, or a bad flag print the usage message and exit 1.
Otherwise `getconf` exits 0.

# BUGS

`pathconf` in musl looks at no file: each value is the same for every <path>, which need not exist.

`OPEN_MAX` and `CHILD_MAX` print `undefined`: the POSIX layer reports every resource limit as infinite.
